<div class="sasha-practice-page">

  <div class="sasha-container">


    <!-- ============================================
         HEADER
    ============================================= -->

    <header class="sasha-practice-page-hero">

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-eyebrow">
        Sasha Best Practice
      </p>

      <h1>
        Proposal Graphics Review
      </h1>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-intro">
        Review the proposal's graphics, photographs, tables,
        diagrams, charts, callouts, and other visual elements
        to make sure they help evaluators understand the
        submission quickly, clearly, and persuasively.
      </p>

    </header>


    <!-- ============================================
         PURPOSE
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Graphics are part of the proposal argument
        </h2>

        <p>
          A proposal is not only a written document.
          It is also a visual communication tool.
        </p>

        <p>
          Graphics should help the evaluator understand
          information faster, recognize relationships,
          compare alternatives, follow processes, see
          evidence, understand responsibilities, recognize
          scale, or find important information.
        </p>

        <p>
          A graphic that does none of these things may be
          decoration rather than communication.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            The basic test
          </strong>

          <span>
            What does this visual help the evaluator
            understand?
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         FIRST REVIEW
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Start with the evaluator
        </h2>

        <p>
          Before judging whether a graphic looks good,
          determine what information the evaluator needs
          from it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ask:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            What should the evaluator understand?
          </li>

          <li>
            What relationship needs to be visible?
          </li>

          <li>
            Is the graphic supporting an RFP requirement
            or evaluation criterion?
          </li>

          <li>
            Does it reinforce an important win theme?
          </li>

          <li>
            Does it make evidence easier to recognize?
          </li>

          <li>
            Could the same information be communicated
            more clearly another way?
          </li>

        </ul>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEW STEPS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <h2>
          Conduct the graphics review
        </h2>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-step-list">


        <!-- STEP 1 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            01
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check visual hierarchy
            </h3>

            <p>
              Move quickly through the proposal without
              reading every word.
            </p>

            <p>
              Major sections, important messages, evidence,
              and transitions should still be easy to
              recognize.
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                Are major headings obvious?
              </li>

              <li>
                Can the evaluator see where sections begin?
              </li>

              <li>
                Are important messages visually prominent?
              </li>

              <li>
                Is the page structure consistent?
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 2 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            02
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Match the proposal visually to the RFP
            </h3>

            <p>
              Evaluators should be able to move easily
              between the RFP requirements and the
              corresponding proposal content.
            </p>

            <p>
              Where practical, preserve recognizable RFP
              terminology and make major requested scope
              items visually obvious.
            </p>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                Goal
              </strong>

              <span>
                Do not make evaluators mentally translate
                between the client's structure and your
                structure.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 3 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            03
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check proximity, contrast, alignment, and repetition
            </h3>

            <p>
              Review the proposal using four basic visual
              communication principles.
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                <strong>Proximity:</strong>
                related information should appear together.
              </li>

              <li>
                <strong>Contrast:</strong>
                different levels and types of information
                should be visually distinct.
              </li>

              <li>
                <strong>Alignment:</strong>
                elements should look deliberately placed,
                not scattered.
              </li>

              <li>
                <strong>Repetition:</strong>
                repeated information types should use
                consistent visual treatments.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 4 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            04
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Protect negative space
            </h3>

            <p>
              Empty space is not wasted proposal space.
              It helps evaluators distinguish headings,
              graphics, callouts, sections, and important
              messages.
            </p>

            <p>
              Do not solve page-limit problems by squeezing
              everything together until the document becomes
              difficult to read.
            </p>

            <p>
              Remove weak content before sacrificing
              readability.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 5 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            05
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check whether each graphic type fits the information
            </h3>

            <p>
              Choose the graphic according to the relationship
              the evaluator needs to understand.
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                <strong>Time or sequence:</strong>
                timeline, schedule, roadmap, Gantt chart.
              </li>

              <li>
                <strong>Process:</strong>
                workflow, process diagram, decision tree,
                swimlane.
              </li>

              <li>
                <strong>Comparison:</strong>
                table, matrix, bar chart, side-by-side
                comparison.
              </li>

              <li>
                <strong>Responsibility:</strong>
                organization chart, responsibility matrix,
                role-to-task matrix.
              </li>

              <li>
                <strong>Geography:</strong>
                map, site plan, corridor diagram.
              </li>

              <li>
                <strong>Scale or quantity:</strong>
                quantitative callout, chart, infographic,
                annotated photograph.
              </li>

              <li>
                <strong>Risk:</strong>
                risk-response table, matrix, or mitigation
                graphic.
              </li>

              <li>
                <strong>Evidence:</strong>
                project matrix, metric callout, project map,
                comparison graphic, or annotated photograph.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 6 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            06
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Review tables as graphics
            </h3>

            <p>
              Tables are especially useful when information
              needs to be found, compared, verified, or
              revisited quickly.
            </p>

            <p>
              Consider tables for:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                compliance;
              </li>

              <li>
                project experience;
              </li>

              <li>
                personnel qualifications;
              </li>

              <li>
                scope and responses;
              </li>

              <li>
                risks and mitigations;
              </li>

              <li>
                deliverables;
              </li>

              <li>
                responsibilities;
              </li>

              <li>
                assumptions;
              </li>

              <li>
                schedules; and
              </li>

              <li>
                client benefits.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 7 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            07
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Review photography as evidence
            </h3>

            <p>
              The best proposal photograph is not necessarily
              the most beautiful photograph.
            </p>

            <p>
              Ask:
            </p>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                What does this photograph prove?
              </strong>

              <span>
                Does it demonstrate scale, complexity,
                operating conditions, stakeholder activity,
                specialized equipment, construction staging,
                completed quality, or another point relevant
                to the proposal?
              </span>

            </div>

            <p>
              Avoid photographs that exist only because the
              page had an empty space.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 8 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            08
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Review every caption
            </h3>

            <p>
              Captions should contribute to the proposal.
              Do not waste them merely identifying something
              the evaluator can already see.
            </p>

            <p>
              Instead of:
            </p>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                Project A
              </strong>

              <span>
                explain what the image proves and why that
                evidence matters to this pursuit.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 9 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            09
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Apply the three-second test
            </h3>

            <p>
              Look at each major graphic briefly.
            </p>

            <p>
              Ask:
            </p>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                If an evaluator looked at this for three seconds,
                what would they understand?
              </strong>

              <span>
                If the main message is unclear, simplify the
                graphic.
              </span>

            </div>

            <p>
              The visual may contain deeper information for
              someone who studies it longer, but its central
              message should be immediately apparent.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 10 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            10
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Remove decoration
            </h3>

            <p>
              Review every visual element that does not appear
              to communicate useful information.
            </p>

            <p>
              Question:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                meaningless stock photography;
              </li>

              <li>
                decorative technical drawings;
              </li>

              <li>
                unnecessary icons;
              </li>

              <li>
                charts without a meaningful comparison;
              </li>

              <li>
                unrelated photographs;
              </li>

              <li>
                diagrams that merely repeat headings;
              </li>

              <li>
                elaborate infographics with little substance;
                and
              </li>

              <li>
                graphics too complicated to understand
                quickly.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <p>
              Professional design should support the proposal
              argument, not compete with it.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         GRAPHIC BRIEFS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Write a graphic brief before designing
        </h2>

        <p>
          When the review identifies a new or revised
          graphic, describe what the designer needs to
          communicate before anyone starts drawing.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Purpose
            </strong>

            <span>
              What should the evaluator understand?
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Graphic type
            </strong>

            <span>
              Timeline, process diagram, table, chart,
              map, photograph, organization chart, or
              another appropriate form.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Core message
            </strong>

            <span>
              What is the single most important point?
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Information to include
            </strong>

            <span>
              Identify the data, phases, people, values,
              labels, or evidence required.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Information to exclude
            </strong>

            <span>
              Remove anything that adds clutter without
              strengthening the message.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Connection to strategy
            </strong>

            <span>
              Explain why the visual matters to this
              pursuit and win strategy.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Heading or caption
            </strong>

            <span>
              State what the evaluator should understand
              from the visual.
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         EFFORT LEVEL
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Match graphics effort to proposal effort
        </h2>

        <div class="sasha-practice-fact-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

            <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
              Minimal
            </p>

            <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
              Keep it simple
            </p>

            <p>
              Use existing photography, simple tables,
              and easily produced graphics where they
              materially improve clarity or compliance.
            </p>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

            <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
              Usual
            </p>

            <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
              Tailor important visuals
            </p>

            <p>
              Develop proposal-specific graphics for
              important methodology, team, experience,
              evidence, or evaluator-facing messages.
            </p>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

            <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
              Full
            </p>

            <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
              Use graphics strategically
            </p>

            <p>
              Actively identify opportunities for custom
              visual communication throughout the proposal,
              especially where graphics reinforce the win
              strategy or simplify complex information.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         CONSISTENCY
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Maintain visual consistency
        </h2>

        <p>
          The finished proposal should look like one document,
          even when several people have contributed to it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Check for consistent treatment of:
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Headings</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Project examples</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Personnel profiles</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Callouts</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Tables</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Captions</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Charts</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Timelines</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Risk graphics</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Benefits and evidence</span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         SASHA ROLE
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div>

          <h2>
            What Sasha can help review
          </h2>

        </div>

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Sasha does not need to replace your proposal
            designer or graphic specialist.
          </p>

          <p>
            She can help identify where information would
            communicate better visually, recommend the right
            graphic type, test whether existing graphics
            communicate clearly, develop graphic briefs,
            strengthen captions, and identify visuals that
            are adding clutter instead of value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sometimes the best graphics recommendation is
            fewer words and a better visual.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         FINAL REVIEW
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Final graphics review
        </h2>

        <p>
          Before considering the visual review complete,
          ask:
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Does every major graphic have a clear purpose?
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Can important messages be understood while skimming?
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Do visuals support the RFP and evaluation criteria?
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Are graphics reinforcing the win strategy?
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Are captions contributing evidence or meaning?
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Are charts and diagrams simpler than the prose they replace?
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Is there enough negative space to make the document readable?
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Are repeated visual elements consistent?
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Have decorative or irrelevant visuals been removed?
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Can each important graphic pass the three-second test?
            </span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         RETURN
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <a
          href="/plan{{#if pursuitId}}?pursuit={{pursuitId}}{{/if}}"
        >
          Return to Plan
        </a>

      </div>

    </section>


  </div>

</div>